  "reasoning": "1. The patient reports intense, persistent shivering (chills), a very high fever, and skin manifestations including rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck.\n2. Evaluating against the reference disease profiles: Malaria typically presents with high fever and chills but rarely includes prominent rashes. Typhoid features sustained high fever alongside significant gastrointestinal symptoms (stomach pain, diarrhea/constipation) rather than cutaneous rashes. Chicken pox involves an itchy, bumpy rash and loss of appetite, but severe shivering/chills are not its hallmark.\n3. Dengue classically matches this exact symptom cluster in the training references: sudden high fever, pronounced chills (shivering), body aches, and the appearance of rashes or red spots/patches on the arms, neck, and other body parts. Several dengue examples explicitly combine high fever, chills, and red rashes/spots.\n4. Given the strong alignment of high fever, persistent shivering, and localized red rashes/patches with the dengue reference cases, dengue is the most accurate diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
